    pr_ref:  # 02c0
    description: "Residual of INV-hopib (PR #1340). The filed instance -- print(k, file=sys.stderr) -- is one row now, and a stream written through a call that is not a sink names that call in sink_carriers. Two shapes still produce a stream-object record that names a sink with no arguments and no receiver at the cited line:\n1. AN ALIAS. `out = sys.stderr` then `print(k, file=out)` / `out.write(k)`: the attribute's only use is an assignment, so it carries no attr_carrier (None), the subsumption keeps it (correctly -- nothing vouches for dropping it), and the record names sys.stderr at the assignment line with sink_carriers []. The write happens through `out` on another line. Fix shape (not built): follow the alias's binding to its call uses (the DDG knows `out`'s uses), or stamp the alias's carrier set.\n2. CPP `std::cerr << k`. cpp.py passes no carrier kinds: its stream writes are `<<` binary expressions, not calls, so the record names std.cerr with no carrier. Arguably the stream IS the receiver of operator<< (the statement's receiver clause), but the record does not say so. Fix shape: stamp \"operator<<@line\" when the attribute is the left operand of a `<<` binary_expression (chained: the innermost).\nNeither measured on a corpus. The A/B for #1340 (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/hopib_09302026) left apt and modsecurity unchanged, as pre-registered, because cpp is not wired."  # 02c0
    fields: {}  # 02c0
